Now I have a good understanding of the codebase. Let me analyze the issue:

The issue is about the `stringifyName` function in `src/index.ts` (lines near the end of the file). The function determines whether a parameter name needs to be quoted when stringifying.

```typescript
function stringifyName(name: string, next: Token | undefined): string {
  if (!ID.test(name)) return quoteName(name);

  if (next?.type === "text" && ID_CONTINUE.test(next.value[0])) {
    return quoteName(name);
  }

  return name;
}
```

The problem: When `next.value[0]` is an astral Unicode character (code point > 0xFFFF), `next.value[0]` only returns the high surrogate (a single UTF-16 code unit), not the full character. The regex `ID_CONTINUE` with the `/u` flag operates on Unicode code points, so it won't correctly match a lone surrogate.

For example, if the text following a param is `𐐀` (U+10400, DESERET CAPITAL LETTER LONG I), it's an ID_Continue character, but `next.value[0]` gives `\uD801`, which is NOT an ID_Continue character. So the test fails, and the name is NOT quoted, which breaks the round-trip.

The fix is to properly extract the first code point from the string. We can use `[...str][0]` (spread operator with `/u` flag) or we can use the `codePointAt` approach. Since the regex uses `/u` flag, we should use the spread operator to get the first full code point.

Let me verify this hypothesis with a reproduction script before moving to implementation.

